* ============================================================================
* validate_unique_repair_index_proof.dts
*
* Proves: VALIDATE UNIQUE ... REPAIR maintains the ACTIVE INDEX while it
* rewrites a duplicate key, so the repaired record is reachable through the
* index with NO REINDEX/REBUILD in between.
*
* Why this exists (item C1, session 2026-07-30_cowork_house_index_vdisk):
* the REPAIR loop used A.set() + A.writeCurrent(), which carry no index hook.
* It rewrote a uniqueness-candidate field -- exactly the kind of field likely
* to carry a tag -- and left the index pointing at the old value with nothing
* marking it stale. REPAIR now routes through DbArea::replaceFieldStored(),
* which owns the record lock, the write, and the index replace snapshot.
*
* MUST run on x64/CDX, not x32/CNX. CnxBackend::upsert/erase are still stubs
* (lane XIDX-TXN-02), so a CNX table cannot maintain on mutation at all and
* this proof would fail there for an unrelated reason.
*
* Self-contained: builds and erases its own throwaway table; never touches
* students or any live fixture. Run standalone: DO validate_unique_repair_index_proof
* Regression: REGRESSION RUN VUREPAIR
* Markers: VUR_T2 and VUR_T3 must both print .T. (there is no T1 -- see the
* marker-authoring note below the REPAIR step).
*
* Runtime status: PROVEN 2026-07-30 on the wsl-lean build (Linux, Ninja, LMDB).
* First run: T2 and T3 green, plus engine output "Found at 3." / "Found at 2."
* confirming SEEK landed on the repaired record at its NEW key with no REINDEX
* between. T1 printed empty because of a marker-authoring bug (FOUND() does not
* concatenate); corrected below, and the underlying behaviour it was meant to
* assert was already evidenced by "Found at 3." plus T2.
* ============================================================================
SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

* x64 lane: DBF/INDEXES/LMDB path slots for the CDX-LMDB backend
DO x64

FORMULA "VU-REPAIR-INDEX-PROOF-BEGIN"

* --- throwaway table with a duplicated key value ---
* SID 10, 20, 20, 30. Rec 3 is the duplicate REPAIR must renumber.
* compute_max_numeric_value() sees 30, so rec 3 becomes 31 -- deterministic.
CREATE X64 VUREP (SID N(6), LNAME C(20))
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 10
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ADAMS"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 20
REPLACE LNAME WITH "MILLER"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 20
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ZEBRA"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 30
REPLACE LNAME WITH "YOUNG"

* --- index the uniqueness-candidate field and make it the active order ---
CDX CREATE
CDX ADDTAG SID
REINDEX CDX
SET ORDER TAG SID

* --- repair. NOTE: no REINDEX after this line, deliberately. ---
VALIDATE UNIQUE FIELD SID REPAIR

* --- T1: the repaired record is reachable through the index at its NEW key ---
* Before the fix the index still carried 20 for this record, so SEEK 31 missed.
*
* MARKER-AUTHORING NOTE, learned the hard way (two failed attempts 2026-07-30):
* "? <string> + <logical>" renders the logical ONLY when the logical came from a
* STRING comparison. Both of these printed the label with NO value at all:
*     ? "x:" + FOUND()                 && function returning logical -> empty
*     ? "x:" + (RECNO() = 3)           && numeric comparison         -> empty
* while (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA") renders .T. correctly. Use string comparisons
* for markers, or the assertion silently reads as a failure when it is not.
*
* There is deliberately no T1 marker now. It was redundant: if SEEK 31 missed,
* the record pointer would not be on the repaired row and T2 below would read
* .F. So T2 proves both "found" and "found the right record" in one assertion,
* and the engine's own "Found at 3." line is the corroborating evidence.
SEEK 31

* --- T2: and it is the right record ---
? "VUR_T2_seek_new_key_is_ZEBRA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

* --- T3: the surviving legitimate 20 is still findable and is MILLER ---
* Guards the other direction: the fix must not have erased the wrong entry
* from the equal-key run.
SEEK 20
? "VUR_T3_seek_old_key_is_MILLER:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "MILLER")

FORMULA "VU-REPAIR-INDEX-PROOF-END"

* --- teardown ---
* No bare "SET ORDER TO" here: it reports "SET ORDER: missing target."
* CLOSE drops the order with the table.
CLOSE
ERASE VUREP CONFIRM
